## Why This Problem Is Asked

This problem explores non-overlapping palindromic substring selection using a two-dimensional DP that tracks prefix length and the number of selected substrings. It tests palindrome precomputation, transition logic, and solution reconstruction under constraints.

## Key Concepts

- **Palindrome table**: pal[l][r] indicates whether s[l..r] is a palindrome
- **DP state**: dp[i][t] represents the maximum total length obtainable from prefix s[0..i] using exactly t substrings
- **Transition**: Either skip the current position or end a palindrome at i (starting at some l)
- **Reconstruction**: Use parent pointers to backtrack and retrieve the selected substrings

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPalindromes(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const pal = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pal[i][i] = true;
  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let l = 0; l + len - 1 < n; l++) {
      const r = l + len - 1;
      if (s[l] === s[r] && (len === 2 || pal[l+1][r-1])) {
        pal[l][r] = true;
      }
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1]?.[t] ?? 0;
      parent[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let l = 0; l <= i; l++) {
        if (pal[l][i]) {
          const prev = l > 0 ? dp[l-1][t-1] : 0;
          const val = prev + (i - l + 1);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            parent[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t-1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (i >= 0 && t > 0) {
    const choice = parent[i][t];
    if (!choice.skip) {
      result.unshift([choice.l, i]);
      t = choice.t;
      i = choice.l - 1;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { maxLength: dp[n-1][k], substrings: result };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem assesses your ability to handle non-trivial path constraints in dynamic programming, specifically exact turn counting. It also requires path reconstruction, reflecting real-world interview complexity where identifying a solution isn't sufficient—you must also provide the actual path.

## Key Concepts
- **4D DP state** capturing both position and path history: `(i, j, t, dir)` tracks where you are, how many turns you've made, and your incoming direction
- **Turn counting** via conditional transitions: staying in the same direction doesn't increment the turn count, while switching does
- **Backpointer-based reconstruction** stores the predecessor for each state, enabling full path recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [-Infinity, -Infinity])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [null, null])
    )
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromRight = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromDown = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : -Infinity;
          if (fromRight >= fromDown) {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t, 0];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t-1, 1];
          }
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromDown = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromRight = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : -Infinity;
          if (fromDown >= fromRight) {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t, 1];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t-1, 0];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const endDir = dp[n-1][m-1][k][0] >= dp[n-1][m-1][k][1] ? 0 : 1;
  const maxSum = dp[n-1][m-1][k][endDir];
  const path = [];
  let cur = [n-1, m-1, k, endDir];

  while (cur) {
    path.unshift([cur[0], cur[1]]);
    cur = parent[cur[0]][cur[1]][cur[2]][cur[3]];
  }

  return { sum: maxSum, path };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a DP with a non-trivial cost function and prefix optimization.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes and partitions
- O(1) substring cost via precomputed diff/pref
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute diff and prefix
let diff = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) diff[i] = s[i-1] !== s[i-2] ? 1:0;
let pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i];

// cost(a,b)
function cost(a,b){ return pref[b] - pref[a]; }

// DP
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = cost(1,i); }

for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
  for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
    for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + cost(p+1,i);
      if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

